We are sorry for the late response. Below are our answers to your questions. We hope our answers clarify our paper and we obtain more suggestions from you.

&nbsp;

Q1) Assumption about homogeneous hardware and homogeneous network.

A1) Our paper focuses on homogenous hardware and network. Under the hardware homogeneity, the local training time is proportional to the number of mini-batches. Base on assumptions of hardware and network homogeneity, we formulate the system overheads and propose a tuning algorithm. We agree that in practice devices are heterogeneous.  However, we believe our paper provides a new perspective of FL hyper-parameter tuning and has a good potential to inspire much research on this new research direction.  We plan to add an experiment to verify that our method can also work for other aggregation algorithms such as FedNova.

&nbsp;

Q2) Having an accurate benchmark of the application that requires tuning essentially requires executing the entire learning before the tuning is made possible. This makes the entire merit of the proposal questionable.

A2) We want to highlight that our benchmark (measurement) study is to illustrate the FL performance w.r.t. M and E. Our results in Table 2 are consistent with existing papers, but existing papers do not conduct these measurements as comprehensively as we do to cover all system aspects. We DO NOT require measurement before applying FedTuning to new FL applications.

&nbsp; 

Q3) With GBoard being distributed to millions of devices (as the authors also state) an application with 2618 clients cannot be characterised as “massively distributed”. However, given the scarcity of open-source datasets it is a reasonable choice for a dataset.

A3) We will also explore other datasets that could be helpful and meaningful on FL hyper-parameter tuning. 

&nbsp; 

Q4)  Why does ResNet-26 not introduce any accuracy improvements ? Are there any published baselines for these network/dataset combinations ?

A4) Model accuracy does not necessarily increase with the more deep model, e.g., the infamous vanishing gradient problem. We may need more advanced model structures. There are many works to improve the model accuracy for the speech-to-command dataset in centralized machine learning, but not in the federate learning setting. In an OSDI'21 paper called Oort (https://www.usenix.org/conference/osdi21/presentation/lai), the authors set a target accuracy of 62.2% (Table 2), whereas we set a much higher accuracy of 80%.

Thanks for your time and helpful suggestions. We would like to answer reviewers' concerns to clarify and further improve our paper.

&nbsp;

A1-1) Currently only E and M are tuned, which is low dimensionality (E has only 5 choices between 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8 and M has only 4 choices 1,10, 20, 50), which is very small in total 20 choices.

Q1-1) In our measurement study we use these M and E values to illustrate the FL performance against M and E. The measurement study leads to Table 2, which our algorithm is relied on. The measurement results are consistent with existing papers, but existing papers do not conduct these measurements as comprehensively as we do to cover all system aspects. As you can see our evaluation results in Table 3 regarding the final M and final E, we do not constrain M and E values. 

&nbsp;

A1-2) Using the proposed method seems to treat each parameter independently via optimizing the reward function, could ignore the interplay between multiple hyperparameters

Q1-2) There are interplays between multiple hyperparameters, as Figure 2 shows that the performance is dependent on both M and E. However, we always observe a consistent trend of the system performance over hyper-parameters. For example, when E is fixed (no matter what is its value as long it remains in a practical range), the time is reduced with a larger M. We admit that our measurement study is not exhaustive (only a range of M and E are studied because of limited computation power), however, our measurement results are consistent with existing empirical and theoretical papers on different system aspects. 

&nbsp;

Q2) Comparison with other Hyper-parameter Optimization (HPO) 

A2) It would be greatly appreciated if the reviewer can be more explicit about which HPO algorithm can be applied in our scenario. We would like to compare ours with theirs to make our paper stronger. Our FL scenario has the following challenges/characteristics: (1) No global objective function exists to include different system aspects (time, byte, FLOP, etc), therefore, we design the comparison function in Eq. (5) to compare two FL sets; (2) Online decision and there is only one instance because the training has to continue, otherwise, it causes much higher system training costs (3) Different stages (early vs late) of training prefer different M and E, and thus the HPO has to be adaptive to the training stages. 

&nbsp;

Q3) Experiments are far from enough. The only dataset is Google speech-to-command.

A3) We are currently collecting more experiment results. 

&nbsp;

Q4) Extending the solution to more hyperparameters are not clear.

A4) We only consider the hyper-parameters that are unique in FL. For other hyper-parameters such as learning rate, they have already been extensively studied.

Thanks for your time and helpful suggestions. We would like to answer reviewers' concerns to clarify and further improve our paper. 

&nbsp;

Q1) Degraded performances in some cases.

A1) $\frac{\partial t_{nxt}}{\partial M}$ can be approximated by $(+1) \times |t_{nxt} - t_{cur}|$. However, in our submission version, we use $|t_{cur} - t_{prv}|$ to estimate $|t_{nxt} - t_{cur}|$, which is not always accurate. Instead, we update our algorithm for the $|t_{nxt} - t_{cur}|$ estimation by $\eta_{t-1} \times |t_{cur} - t_{prv}|$ where $\eta_{t-1} = \frac{|t_{cur} - t_{prv}|}{|t_{prv} - t_{prvprv}|}$. Similar changed are made for other system aspects. We found these small changes can greatly improve the overall performance (and solve these degraded cases).

&nbsp;

Q2) Related works that consider time, computation and communication altogether

A2) Thanks for introducing these two works to us. These two papers you mentioned target round-to-accuracy performance. For example, the model accuracy after 100 rounds in [1]. Round-to-accuracy is closely related to the transmission time if a homogenous network is assumed. Considering that transmission time is important, we would add the transmission time in our updated version. The transmission time can be formulated by $C_4 \times R$, since it is dependent on the number of training rounds. It can be easily integrated into our updated version. 

&nbsp;

Q3) The third issue pertains to wording and sentence structures. There were grammatical and syntactic errors which obscured the clarity of the manuscript. 

A3) We will carefully proofread our paper. 

&nbsp;

Q4) It would have been helpful (or better) to discuss literature specifically on automated hyper-parameter tuning methods for FL algorithms, then connect that literature to their proposed automated hyper-parameter tuning method. 

A4) We agree that we should mention more related work (although there are few) on FL hyper-parameter tuning. In addition, we plan to emphasize the difference of HPO for FL compared to HPO for others.
